# hexcat — hex text <-> raw bytes, both directions.  The smoke test for M-TOOL.
#
#   gtools/build gtools/hexcat.golfj build/hexcat
#   build/hexcat encode < in.hex > out.bin      hex text  ->  raw bytes
#   build/hexcat decode < in.bin > out.hex      raw bytes ->  hex text
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It is the smallest program that exercises the whole wave-8 stack at once: the
# ⎈ syscall (through every tio wrapper), the entry-rsp cell (through argv), all
# three tool libraries, and gtools/build itself.  If hexcat round-trips a real
# binary, the foundation under encode/decode/mkblob/mkgolf2 is sound; if it does
# not, the failure is here, in thirty lines, instead of inside a table-driven
# encoder.  It is also the reference for what a tool in this directory looks
# like — read this one before writing another.
#
# Unlike its siblings hexcat shadows NO Python tool, so test/gtools.sh cannot
# check it differentially.  It is checked as a ROUND TRIP instead: decode then
# encode must reproduce the input byte for byte, over real binaries (build/golf2
# itself, self/golf2.golf, data/blob.hex).  That is a weaker oracle than
# "identical to Python" — a reader and a writer that are wrong in exactly
# mirrored ways would still round-trip — so the suite also pins the exact output
# text for a known input and checks every error path.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SUBCOMMAND NAMES, and why they point the way they do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# They are tools/codepage.py's, with tools/codepage.py's meaning:
#
#     encode   the human-readable form  ->  raw bytes      (hex text -> binary)
#     decode   raw bytes  ->  the human-readable form      (binary -> hex text)
#
# For codepage.py the readable form is glyph/mnemonic source; here it is a hex
# dump, of which ../data/blob.hex is the repo's own example.  So `hexcat encode`
# is the direction that CONSUMES text and `hexcat decode` is the one that
# PRODUCES it, exactly as with codepage.py, and a reader who knows one knows the
# other.  (Read the other way round — "encode this binary as hex" — the names
# would be backwards, which is why the usage message spells both out.)
# A round trip is therefore `decode` and then `encode`.
#
# Both names being six letters is a small bonus the argv compare below leans on.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INPUT FORMAT (what `encode` accepts) — deliberately close to data/blob.hex
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A byte is TWO ADJACENT hex digits, upper or lower case.  Between bytes:
#
#   * whitespace — the space, and bytes 9..13 (TAB LF VT FF CR) — any amount;
#   * a `#` comment, from the `#` through the end of that line, skipped.
#
# "Adjacent" is the one rule worth stating twice: `4A` is one byte, `4 A` is an
# error, not two half-bytes.  data/blob.hex writes pairs and separates them with
# single spaces, so the format it uses is the format this reads, comment header
# included — `hexcat encode < data/blob.hex` yields the template blob (bar the
# terminating 0 sentinel, which that file deliberately omits).
#
# Everything else is fatal, on fd 2 with a non-zero status:
#   * a stray digit at the very end          "odd number of hex digits"
#   * anything that is not a hex digit,
#     whitespace or `#`                      "expected two hex digits"
# A NUL or any other control byte falls in the second case on purpose: the
# whitespace test names the four bytes it allows rather than taking every byte
# below ' ', so binary fed to `encode` by mistake is diagnosed and not silently
# eaten.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OUTPUT FORMAT (what `decode` writes)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two UPPERCASE hex digits per byte, single spaces between them, a newline every
# 16 bytes and after the last one; no trailing space anywhere, and empty input
# gives an empty file.  That is data/blob.hex's shape as closely as a filter can
# get it — that file breaks lines at record boundaries, which is structure
# hexcat cannot see, so a fixed 16 is the honest substitute.  The one hard
# requirement is that this output is accepted by `encode` above, and it is: only
# pairs, spaces and newlines come out.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS REPLACES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A twelve-line prototype, written against ⎈ before the tool libraries existed,
# proved the hex->bin direction was reachable at all:
#
#     :n →c ←c 97 <1+ [ ←c 65 <1+ [ ←c 48 - ^ ] ←c 55 - ^ ] ←c 87 - ;
#     { ( →a  ←a [ 0 . ]  ←a 32 \gt 1+ [ ←a n 16 * ( n + ) ]  0 }
#
# Every line of it is now somebody else's job: `(` and `)` — one read syscall
# and one write syscall PER BYTE — are `e` and `g`, the hand-rolled nibble
# decoder is ttext's `k` and `l`, and the missing halves (an error path, a
# direction flag, the reverse direction) are the reason this file is not twelve
# lines.  It is kept above as the historical note it is.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONVENTIONS — the same ones lib/tio.golfj and lib/ttext.golfj document
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authored in code-page mnemonic form (\radd, \fetch, \sys …); the one glyph left
# as a glyph is the string delimiter “, for the same reason those files leave it.
# All pointer, index and flag arithmetic uses the raw atoms \radd \rsub \rmul
# \rmin \rlt, never the bare ASCII + - * ⌈ ⌊, which M-VEC made polymorphic.
# `\and \or / % ≡ ≺ ? ,` are not hooked and stay bare.  Byte 0x5C (swap)
# never appears.  `{…}` is a DO-while, so both scan loops are guarded with
# `<len> 0\eq [ {…} ]` and empty input walks straight past them.
#
# WORDS.  ../REGISTRY.md §2.1 leaves a gtools/ program the letters no prelude
# word and no tool library claims — which is `B` and `C`, and nothing else — so
# this file spends both and inlines everything smaller:
#     B   ->      encode: hex text on stdin -> raw bytes on stdout
#     C   ->      decode: raw bytes on stdin -> hex text on stdout
# The nibble-to-digit conversion is therefore written out twice inside `C`
# rather than becoming a third word.  It is one line and it is commented once.
#
# VARIABLES ARE DIGITS.  §2.1 gives a gtools/ program "any letter not claimed
# above, plus digits", and after `B` and `C` no letter is left, so the six
# variables here are named `0`–`5` (`\set0` is `→0`, `\get0` is `←0`):
#     0   the input buffer — a heap address handed back by tio's `e`
#     1   its length in bytes
#     2   the cursor: how far into the buffer we have got
#     3   the byte under the cursor
#     4   a nibble (in `C`), or the separator byte about to be written
#     5   in `B`, the "this byte is still unclaimed" flag of the three-way
#         comment / whitespace / hex-pair dispatch
# Both words use the same numbers for the same things.  Every one of them holds
# a small non-negative count, an index, a byte, or a heap address — nothing
# negative and nothing that could reach 2^31 — and that is deliberate, not luck.
#
# NOTHING NEGATIVE EVER REACHES THE BANK.  ../REGISTRY.md §2.1 records what a
# sibling unit lost a segfault to: a bank cell was FOUR bytes and `\get`
# zero-extended, so a -1 parked in one came back as 4294967295, the `0≺`
# not-found branch silently did not fire, and a loop bound derived from it ran
# about four billion times.  The one -1 in this file — `l`'s "not a hex digit" —
# is therefore tested while it is still on the DATA stack, never through a
# variable.  Keep that shape even as the bank's width moves under it: a marker
# tested where it was produced cannot be truncated by any cell size, so the rule
# costs nothing and stops depending on what the bank happens to be this week.
#
# ARGV POINTERS NEVER GO IN A VARIABLE EITHER.  `argv[1]` is a stack address,
# far above 2^32.  It stays on the data stack for the whole dispatch below,
# which is why that reads as one expression.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# B — encode: hex text on stdin -> raw bytes on stdout.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One pass, one step per iteration, three-way: a `#` comment, a run of
# whitespace, or a pair of hex digits.  GOLF has no `else`, so the arms are
# chained on `5` — set to 1 by whichever arm claims the byte, and each following
# arm guarded by `\get5[…]`, which runs only while it is still 0.
:B 0 e\set1\set0                        # slurp stdin: e -> buf len, so 1 then 0
 0\set2
 \get1 0\eq[{
  \get0 \get2\radd?\set3                # 3 = the byte at the cursor
  0\set5                                # nobody has claimed it yet
  # `#` … end of line.  The scan reads through \rmin against len-1 so a file
  # whose last line has no newline walks off nothing; it stops on the newline
  # or on the end, and the \radd after it steps over whichever one it found.
  \get3 35\rsub[
   {\get2 1\radd\set2
    \get2 \get1\rlt1\radd
    \get0 \get2 \get1 1\rsub\rmin\radd?10\eq\or}
   \get2 1\radd\set2 1\set5]
  # Whitespace: space, or one of TAB LF VT FF CR.  `(c-9) <u 5` covers the run
  # at 9 and `c-32` is 0 only for the space; \rmin of the two 0/1-or-huge
  # results is 0 exactly when one of them said yes.  Naming the allowed bytes
  # rather than testing `c < 33` is what makes a stray NUL an error below.
  \get5[\get3 9\rsub 5\rlt1\radd \get3 32\rsub\rmin[\get2 1\radd\set2 1\set5]]
  # Otherwise it has to be two hex digits.  The bounds check comes first because
  # `l` reads addr and addr+1 unconditionally: `i+1 <u len` is -1 while there is
  # a second digit to read, so `[` fires exactly when there is not.  Then `l`,
  # whose -1 is tested on the data stack and never through a variable.
  \get5[
   \get2 1\radd \get1\rlt[“hexcat: odd number of hex digits“a]
   \get0 \get2\radd l"0\slt1\radd[“hexcat: expected two hex digits“a]g
   \get2 2\radd\set2]
  \get2 \get1\rlt1\radd}]
 f;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# C — decode: raw bytes on stdin -> hex text on stdout.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nibble to digit, branchlessly: d = n + 48 - 7*(9 <u n).  \rlt yields -1, so
# a nibble of 10..15 picks up the extra +7 that lands it on 'A'..'F' and a digit
# does not — the same trick tutf's `u` uses in the other direction.
:C 0 e\set1\set0
 0\set2
 \get1 0\eq[{
  \get0 \get2\radd?\set3
  \get3 4\shr\set4 \get4 48\radd 9\get4\rlt 7\rmul\rsub g      # the high nibble
  \get3 15\and\set4 \get4 48\radd 9\get4\rlt 7\rmul\rsub g     # the low nibble
  \get2 1\radd\set2                     # 2 is now the count of bytes written
  # A space between bytes, a newline every 16 and after the very last one.
  # Writing the separator into 4 first is how one branch does the work of two:
  # GOLF has no `else`, but a default that a guard overwrites needs none.
  32\set4
  \get2 16%[10\set4]
  \get2 \get1\rsub[10\set4]
  \get4 g
  \get2 \get1\rlt1\radd}]
 f;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The direction flag.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `h` answers 0 when there is no argv[1], which is the whole argc check.  The
# pointer then stays on the data stack under both compares — `"` copies it for
# each — because a variable would truncate it.
#
# A name matches when `p` says the first six bytes are the subcommand AND the
# seventh is the terminating NUL, so `encodeX` is rejected rather than accepted
# as a prefix.  Reading argv[1][6] is only safe because the `p` already passed:
# six bytes matched, so a seventh byte exists.  `p` itself stops at the first
# mismatching byte, so a SHORTER argument — `en` — is never read past its NUL.
#
# `0.` is exit(0): a matched arm must not fall through into the next compare,
# and there is nothing left to do once the direction has run and flushed.
1 h
"[_“usage: hexcat encode < in.hex > out.bin, or hexcat decode < in.bin > out.hex“a]
"6“encode“4\radd 6 p["6\radd?[_ B 0.]]
"6“decode“4\radd 6 p["6\radd?[_ C 0.]]
_“hexcat: first argument must be encode or decode“a
